#somatic_page(
  title: "Welcome to the Depths",
  body_text: [
    #rect(fill: rgb("#F5F5F0"), stroke: 0.5pt + gray, inset: 15pt, radius: 2pt)[
      You may feel a tightening in your chest as you open this journal. Perhaps a subtle resistance, a voice that whispers, _"Not now. Not yet."_ This is not fear of the work itself—it is your psyche protecting you from what Carl Jung called the Shadow: the repository of everything you have disowned, rejected, or hidden from yourself.
      
      The Shadow is not evil. It is not your enemy. It is the unintegrated parts of your own soul—buried not because they are bad, but because at some point in your life, it felt safer to hide them than to be whole. A child learns quickly: *this part of me is too much, too loud, too needy, too angry, too brilliant.* And so, piece by piece, you fragment yourself. You exile the parts that do not fit, and they sink into the unconscious, where they do not disappear—they fester, project, and sabotage.
      
      This journal is an act of *individuation*—Jung's term for the lifelong process of becoming who you truly are. It is not self-help. It is self-reclamation. It is archeology of the soul, conducted not through the mind alone, but through the *soma*—the living, breathing, feeling body that remembers what the mind has learned to forget.
      
      You are here because something within you knows: *it is time.*
    ]
    
    #v(1cm)
    
    #align(center)[
      ◈ · ✦ · ◈
